Overall verdict

The core month-end procedures are recognizable and mostly plausible, but the integration section contains a decisive chronology failure and several accounting statements that would make an experienced controller or healthcare accountant pause. A29_Finance_Month_End_Close_Procedures_Excerpt.docx reads like a later integration-specific supplement inserted into an older policy without updating its revision history.

Highest-confidence concerns

Impossible document chronology
 The procedure is revised and approved in July 2024, but Section 5 says project code INT-WI-2025 was established on the March 3, 2025 acquisition close date. It also governs a subsidiary that was not acquired until that date. A July 2024 document cannot describe those later events as completed facts without a subsequent revision or separately dated supplement.

“Off-balance-sheet accruals” is contradictory terminology
 Section 1.2 places “off-balance-sheet accruals” within the consolidated close. An accrual recognized in the general ledger normally affects financial-statement accounts. Off-balance-sheet commitments or contingencies are a different concept. Finance personnel would likely flag this phrase immediately.

Quarter-end deadlines conflict with later sign-off language
 Section 1.4 extends March, June, and September closes to BD+7. Section 6.3 still requires final Controller sign-off on BD+5 without preserving the quarter-end exception. The earlier Q1 cost email used BD+7, so the document set resolves this inconsistently.

Acquisition accounting is largely absent
 The integration-cost section places systems work, data migration, software, legal, valuation, retention, severance, equipment, facility, and advisory costs into one project code, but does not distinguish expenses from capitalizable implementation costs, acquired assets, transaction costs, restructuring costs, or other purchase-accounting treatment. A project code can collect all costs, but eligibility for the code is not the same as accounting classification.

Closing the project code cannot create a “residual balance” to reclassify
 A project code is described as a cost-collection segment, not an account holding a balance. Closing or archiving it should prevent future use, not require reclassification of all accumulated historical expense. Section 5.6 sounds as though the project code itself is a balance-sheet account.

Healthcare-finance plausibility issues
Patient accounting freeze is too absolute. The document freezes patient accounting at 23:59 on the final calendar day. Healthcare charge capture commonly continues after service dates, requiring late-charge, discharged-not-final-billed, unbilled revenue, coding, and cutoff processes. The procedure mentions accruals generally but does not explain how services rendered before month-end but coded or billed afterward are recognized.
Patient revenue reconciliation is underspecified. Gross charges, contractual allowances, reserves, denials, takebacks, and net-revenue-rate analysis are credible components, but the procedure omits unbilled accounts, discharged-not-final-billed activity, late charges, medical-necessity or coding holds, charity-care classification, and revenue-estimation methodology. This makes the section feel like a summary rather than a controllable procedure.
Post-payment audit recognition is too categorical. The document requires all takebacks, recoupments, or refunds to be recorded when the payer’s final determination is issued. Depending on the facts, an organization may need to recognize an estimate or contingency earlier. The policy leaves no room for probability, estimability, appeal posture, or accounting review.
The AR allowance terminology is muddled. It combines aged AR, allowance for uncollectible accounts, contractual allowances, payer classes, charity care, bad debt, denials, and refunds without clearly separating valuation allowances, contractual adjustments, implicit price concessions, credit balances, and administrative write-offs.
The Chief Compliance Officer has unusual control over project-code closure. Compliance may confirm completion of compliance workstreams, but requiring the CCO to confirm completion of the entire integration before Finance closes the code is unusual. Integration governance, the CFO, or an executive steering committee would more naturally own overall completion.
General finance-control issues
Approval limits use the “absolute value of the entry,” but do not specify whether that means total debits, total credits, largest line, or net impact. For a balanced journal, “net value” would always be zero.
The Controller has unlimited approval authority, yet CFO approval is required above $2.5 million. That makes “unlimited” misleading.
Recurring entries inherit the original approver’s authority indefinitely. A mature control would usually require periodic template recertification and review of continued appropriateness.
Every late item below a threshold is pushed into the following period. That is too mechanical because aggregate misstatement, account-specific risk, recurring cutoff problems, and qualitative materiality can matter even below fixed thresholds.
The 15 percent accrual-variance rule applies the same way to utilities, professional fees, supplies, and contracted services despite their different estimation bases.
Requiring advance approval for every deviation is unrealistic when unexpected close issues arise after the fact. The document does not distinguish prospective exceptions from emergency exceptions and retrospective ratification.
Seven-year retention is plausible as a baseline, but the document does not identify the governing retention schedule or distinguish permanent financial records from supporting close workpapers.
Strongest double-take items
A July 2024-approved procedure describing a March 2025 acquisition and project code as completed events.
“Off-balance-sheet accruals.”
BD+7 quarter-end close conflicting with mandatory BD+5 Controller sign-off.
One integration code covering costs with materially different accounting treatment.
Reclassifying a project code’s “residual balance” when the code closes.
Freezing patient accounting at month-end without a credible late-charge and unbilled-revenue process.
Recognition of every payer audit adjustment only upon final determination.
Compliance effectively determining when the overall integration accounting code may close.




