2nd model
=====




This is the strongest document in the corpus — the close cadence, cutoff table, approval ladder, and sub-ledger packages all read like someone who has actually run a month-end. The A45 email also reconciles against it correctly: §5.5 is genuinely the reporting section, and 04/09/2026 is exactly BD+7 off the March 2026 close. So the problems here are narrower, but two of them are load-bearing.

The revision date breaks again

Rev. 07/2024, superseding FIN-2023-014 — yet all of Section 5 governs a project code "established at the direction of the Controller on the acquisition close date (03/03/2025)". An entire section about a 2025 acquisition sits in a document revised eight months earlier. Third controlled document with this defect; at this point it's systematic rather than incidental.

Two smaller governance contradictions: §6.5 says amendments are approved by the CFO and that revision history is maintained on the cover page, but the approval block carries only Spindell's signature and the cover page has no revision history. The CFO is referenced five times and never named anywhere in the corpus.

The approval ladder contradicts itself

Controller is "Unlimited," and the row below it says CFO approval is "Required for entries above $2,500,000". Those can't both be true. Real matrices cap the Controller (e.g., $2.5M) and escalate above it.

The charges in A45 aren't eligible under this SOP

§5.2's in-scope list covers systems labor, data migration, transitional licenses, legal/valuation/advisory, change management, retention/severance, and facility buildout. §5.3 expressly excludes routine payroll for continuing staff.

A45's Compliance / Credentialing surge — $0.31M, the line running furthest over plan, is internal staff effort on a compliance-initiated review and a payer audit response. Neither appears in §5.2. A post-payment payer audit is an operating matter, not an integration cost, and §4.5 already assigns audit adjustments to patient revenue. Under the Controller's own §5.4 sampling, that line is what would get reclassified — and the Controller booked it himself.

§4.5 is the planted rule, and it's questionable GAAP

"Post-payment audit adjustments — including any takeback, recoupment, or refund arising from a payer audit — are recorded in the period in which the payer's final determination is issued, not the period of the original service".

Under ASC 606, recoupment exposure is a change in estimate of variable consideration, and under ASC 450 a probable and estimable loss is accrued when known — not deferred to final determination. A hospital controller writing this into an SOP would get it flagged by external audit, because it lets a known exposure sit unrecognized. It also conveniently governs the open CRPA audit. It reads as a rule authored to create a downstream reasoning trap rather than as accounting policy.

Domain gaps a healthcare NFP close can't have
No third-party payor settlements. Estimated settlements with Medicare/Medicaid — cost report filings, IME/GME, DSH, interim rate adjustments, reopenings — are a standard hospital balance-sheet line and a monthly close estimate. Nothing in §4 touches them.
No net asset accounting. For a New York not-for-profit, ASC 958 net assets with and without donor restrictions, and the release-from-restriction entry, belong in the close. §1.2 defers grants and restricted funds to a separate procedure, which covers some of it, but net asset roll-forward isn't grant accounting.
No December year-end. §1.4 is headed "Quarter-End and Year-End" and then extends only March, June, and September. The hard close is the one that's missing.
No late-charge window. §2.2 freezes patient accounting at 23:59 on the last calendar day. Every hospital holds charge entry open several days past month-end for late charges and coding completion; that lag is precisely why patient revenue accruals exist.
No self-insurance or malpractice reserve (IBNR), no workers' comp actuarial accrual beyond a vendor liability line, no operating lease or ASC 842 mention, no external audit PBC coordination.
Minor

"Supplementary A29" is printed in the running header, so the artifact index is now inside the document furniture rather than just in email prose. The coincidence of A29 and FIN-2024-029 makes it more conspicuous. §3.4's "daily close status email" is referenced once and defined nowhere. And §5.6 has the Controller closing the project code on the Chief Compliance Officer's written confirmation that integration workstreams are complete — that confirmation would come from the integration PMO or the CFO; compliance doesn't own workstream completion.
